  - [1mThe page doesn't currently work without JavaScript.[0m Every row is built in [38;5;153mactivity.js[39m. Your standing rule says the
    content must stay readable without JS. Filtering is an interactive extra, so it can require JS, but it can't fix
    that existing gap. I'm flagging it rather than expanding scope to cover it.
  - [1mFailed sign-ins matter most.[0m Part 1 decision 7 says the only support tickets so far came from people hunting for a
    failed sign-in they didn't recognize. Whatever filter we add should make that search a single step.

  [1mFirst question: what should people be able to narrow by?[0m

  What actually differs between the options is how much UI and code we add, and whether the failed-sign-in search is one
  step or several.

  - [1mA. Event type only.[0m One [38;5;153m<select>[39m with "All events" plus the nine fixed types. Per 03 Forms, a choice from six or
    more options is a Select. This makes "Sign-in failed" one choice and keeps the diff very small. It doesn't handle
    "what happened last Tuesday" or "anything from Madrid". Adding more filters later is cheap.
  - [1mB. Event type plus date range.[0m Adds two date pickers with presets, as the 03 Forms "Date range" row requires. Dates
    are UTC but have to be shown and compared in the viewer's time zone, which brings in day-boundary edge cases.
    There's also no DatePicker implementation in the repo, so we'd be building one or using native [38;5;153m<input type="date">[39m
    and accepting how it looks. It's useful for an account that's 90 days long, but it roughly triples the work.
  - [1mC. Event type plus a text search over device, location and details.[0m Catches "Madrid" or "Firefox". But location is
    approximate (decision 6), so a search like that can suggest more precision than the data has. The 03 Forms Search
    row also requires a clear button and [38;5;153mautocomplete=off[39m.

  [1mMy recommendation is A.[0m It directly answers the one real complaint, it's the smallest change for a strict review, and
  B or C can be added later without redoing anything.
